1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,312
Sweetheart, you watch movies on a little

2
00:00:03,312 --> 00:00:04,969
phone, lying in bed.

3
00:00:05,149 --> 00:00:06,467
When I was a girl,

4
00:00:06,467 --> 00:00:08,445
we watched them in the car,

5
00:00:08,445 --> 00:00:09,618
under the stars,

6
00:00:09,618 --> 00:00:11,303
on a screen as big as a

7
00:00:11,303 --> 00:00:14,453
barn, with a thousand other families parked

8
00:00:14,453 --> 00:00:15,772
in rows around us.

9
00:00:15,952 --> 00:00:17,821
It was called the drive-in.

10
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:20,140
And on a summer Saturday night in

11
00:00:20,140 --> 00:00:23,188
nineteen fifty-eight, there was no better place

12
00:00:23,188 --> 00:00:23,771
on earth.

13
00:00:23,951 --> 00:00:25,542
Let me take you there.

14
00:00:25,892 --> 00:00:28,560
The first drive-in opened in Camden,

15
00:00:28,560 --> 00:00:31,302
New Jersey, in nineteen thirty-three.

16
00:00:31,482 --> 00:00:34,432
A man named Richard Hollingshead tested it

17
00:00:34,432 --> 00:00:35,837
in his own driveway,

18
00:00:35,837 --> 00:00:38,015
with a projector on the hood of

19
00:00:38,015 --> 00:00:40,404
his car and a sheet nailed between

20
00:00:40,404 --> 00:00:41,106
two trees.

21
00:00:41,286 --> 00:00:43,418
By the late fifties there were more

22
00:00:43,418 --> 00:00:45,977
than four thousand of them across America.

23
00:00:46,157 --> 00:00:48,747
Today there are only about three hundred

24
00:00:48,747 --> 00:00:49,071
left.

25
00:00:49,251 --> 00:00:51,217
Most of the ones I knew are

26
00:00:51,217 --> 00:00:52,746
shopping centers now.

27
00:00:53,096 --> 00:00:56,671
Saturday drive-in night started right after supper.

28
00:00:56,851 --> 00:00:59,262
Mother made a grocery bag full of

29
00:00:59,262 --> 00:01:00,796
popcorn on the stove,

30
00:01:00,796 --> 00:01:03,353
with real butter and too much salt.

31
00:01:03,533 --> 00:01:06,424
She filled a thermos with Kool-Aid and

32
00:01:06,424 --> 00:01:09,543
wrapped a dozen baloney sandwiches in wax

33
00:01:09,543 --> 00:01:10,000
paper.

34
00:01:10,180 --> 00:01:12,483
You could buy food at the snack

35
00:01:12,483 --> 00:01:15,009
bar, but that was for rich people.

36
00:01:15,189 --> 00:01:16,664
We brought our own.

37
00:01:17,014 --> 00:01:19,483
Danny and I put our pajamas on

38
00:01:19,483 --> 00:01:20,717
before we left.

39
00:01:20,897 --> 00:01:22,244
Every kid did.

40
00:01:22,424 --> 00:01:25,506
Because the second movie went past midnight,

41
00:01:25,506 --> 00:01:27,817
and you'd fall asleep in the back

42
00:01:27,817 --> 00:01:30,409
seat, and your father would carry you

43
00:01:30,409 --> 00:01:33,001
into the house without waking you up.

44
00:01:33,181 --> 00:01:35,940
That's the best feeling in the world,

45
00:01:35,940 --> 00:01:38,998
sweetheart, being carried in from the car

46
00:01:38,998 --> 00:01:39,520
asleep.

47
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:42,300
I hope somebody did it for you.

48
00:01:42,651 --> 00:01:44,934
We piled into the Chevrolet,

49
00:01:44,934 --> 00:01:46,401
my father driving,

50
00:01:46,401 --> 00:01:47,951
Mother next to him,

51
00:01:47,951 --> 00:01:50,315
Danny and me in the back with

52
00:01:50,315 --> 00:01:53,088
pillows and a blanket and the dog.

53
00:01:53,268 --> 00:01:55,509
Yes, the dog came.

54
00:01:55,689 --> 00:01:57,500
Skipper loved the drive-in.

55
00:01:57,680 --> 00:01:59,977
And we drove out on Route thirty

56
00:01:59,977 --> 00:02:01,916
to the Springmill Drive-In,

57
00:02:01,916 --> 00:02:03,424
just outside of town,

58
00:02:03,424 --> 00:02:05,937
with the sun going down orange over

59
00:02:05,937 --> 00:02:07,014
the cornfields.

60
00:02:07,365 --> 00:02:09,083
At the gate there was a little

61
00:02:09,083 --> 00:02:10,630
booth with a man in a paper

62
00:02:10,630 --> 00:02:10,859
hat.

63
00:02:11,039 --> 00:02:13,742
A lot of drive-ins charged by the

64
00:02:13,742 --> 00:02:15,625
car, not by the person.

65
00:02:15,805 --> 00:02:18,568
A dollar a carload on Tuesday nights.

66
00:02:18,748 --> 00:02:21,116
On Saturdays it was fifty cents a

67
00:02:21,116 --> 00:02:23,986
person, and children under twelve got in

68
00:02:23,986 --> 00:02:24,344
free.

69
00:02:24,524 --> 00:02:26,793
So my father always said I was

70
00:02:26,793 --> 00:02:27,322
eleven.

71
00:02:27,502 --> 00:02:30,045
I was eleven for about three years.

72
00:02:30,395 --> 00:02:31,770
And the teenagers,

73
00:02:31,770 --> 00:02:33,680
honey, oh, the teenagers.

74
00:02:33,860 --> 00:02:35,818
They'd come in a car with two

75
00:02:35,818 --> 00:02:37,371
boys in the front seat,

76
00:02:37,371 --> 00:02:39,735
and three more hiding in the trunk.

77
00:02:39,915 --> 00:02:42,182
Then they'd park in the back row

78
00:02:42,182 --> 00:02:43,386
and let them out.

79
00:02:43,566 --> 00:02:45,076
Everybody knew.

80
00:02:45,256 --> 00:02:47,694
The man in the paper hat knew.

81
00:02:47,874 --> 00:02:50,526
Sometimes he'd walk around the back of

82
00:02:50,526 --> 00:02:52,689
the car and knock on the trunk,

83
00:02:52,689 --> 00:02:54,015
just to scare them.

84
00:02:54,366 --> 00:02:57,140
My father had his own drive-in rules,

85
00:02:57,140 --> 00:02:57,965
sweetheart.

86
00:02:58,145 --> 00:02:59,567
Park in the middle,

87
00:02:59,567 --> 00:03:01,813
not too close and not too far.

88
00:03:01,993 --> 00:03:04,366
Never next to a car full of

89
00:03:04,366 --> 00:03:05,244
teenagers.

90
00:03:05,424 --> 00:03:07,680
Never next to the snack bar,

91
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:09,372
because of the noise.

92
00:03:09,552 --> 00:03:11,064
And always, always,

93
00:03:11,064 --> 00:03:13,292
back into the spot if it was

94
00:03:13,292 --> 00:03:14,247
a hot night,

95
00:03:14,247 --> 00:03:16,714
so you could open the trunk and

96
00:03:16,714 --> 00:03:18,306
sit on the tailgate.

97
00:03:18,486 --> 00:03:20,784
He'd been doing it since the war.

98
00:03:20,964 --> 00:03:22,961
He never once got it wrong.

99
00:03:23,312 --> 00:03:25,917
Inside, the field was rows and rows

100
00:03:25,917 --> 00:03:27,630
of little gravel hills,

101
00:03:27,630 --> 00:03:29,938
so your car tipped back and you

102
00:03:29,938 --> 00:03:32,395
could see the screen over the car

103
00:03:32,395 --> 00:03:33,586
in front of you.

104
00:03:33,766 --> 00:03:36,008
Every parking spot had a metal pole

105
00:03:36,008 --> 00:03:37,865
with a speaker hanging on it.

106
00:03:38,045 --> 00:03:40,594
You rolled your window down halfway,

107
00:03:40,594 --> 00:03:43,002
hooked the speaker over the glass,

108
00:03:43,002 --> 00:03:44,419
and turned the knob.

109
00:03:44,599 --> 00:03:46,143
The sound was terrible.

110
00:03:46,323 --> 00:03:47,809
Crackly and tinny.

111
00:03:47,989 --> 00:03:49,266
We loved it.

112
00:03:49,616 --> 00:03:52,119
And people forgot to take the speaker

113
00:03:52,119 --> 00:03:53,946
off before they drove away.

114
00:03:54,126 --> 00:03:55,655
Every single night,

115
00:03:55,655 --> 00:03:56,541
sweetheart.

116
00:03:56,721 --> 00:03:58,956
You'd hear a crash of glass and

117
00:03:58,956 --> 00:04:00,037
a man swearing,

118
00:04:00,037 --> 00:04:02,271
and a car window would be lying

119
00:04:02,271 --> 00:04:03,281
in the gravel.

120
00:04:03,461 --> 00:04:06,804
The drive-ins lost thousands of speakers a

121
00:04:06,804 --> 00:04:07,919
year that way.

122
00:04:08,099 --> 00:04:09,991
My father did it once.

123
00:04:10,171 --> 00:04:12,551
Mother never let him forget it.

124
00:04:12,902 --> 00:04:15,442
Before the movie there was a playground

125
00:04:15,442 --> 00:04:16,940
right under the screen,

126
00:04:16,940 --> 00:04:19,415
with swings and a merry-go-round and a

127
00:04:19,415 --> 00:04:20,262
little train.

128
00:04:20,442 --> 00:04:22,482
All the kids ran down there in

129
00:04:22,482 --> 00:04:25,133
their pajamas while it was still light.

130
00:04:25,313 --> 00:04:27,653
You'd swing as high as you could,

131
00:04:27,653 --> 00:04:30,065
and look up at that enormous white

132
00:04:30,065 --> 00:04:31,625
screen over your head,

133
00:04:31,625 --> 00:04:33,541
and wait for the sky to get

134
00:04:33,541 --> 00:04:34,392
dark enough.

135
00:04:34,742 --> 00:04:36,819
Then the lights went down,

136
00:04:36,819 --> 00:04:38,817
and the cartoons came on.

137
00:04:38,997 --> 00:04:41,574
Bugs Bunny, Tom and Jerry.

138
00:04:41,754 --> 00:04:43,305
And then the newsreel,

139
00:04:43,305 --> 00:04:44,715
with the world news.

140
00:04:44,895 --> 00:04:47,735
In nineteen fifty-eight it was all about

141
00:04:47,735 --> 00:04:49,936
the Russians and their Sputnik,

142
00:04:49,936 --> 00:04:52,279
and the first American satellite,

143
00:04:52,279 --> 00:04:54,693
Explorer, that went up in January.

144
00:04:54,873 --> 00:04:57,155
Every newsreel had a rocket in it

145
00:04:57,155 --> 00:04:57,985
that summer.

146
00:04:58,335 --> 00:05:00,239
And then the double feature.

147
00:05:00,419 --> 00:05:02,729
Two movies for the price of one.

148
00:05:02,909 --> 00:05:05,447
The first one was for the family,

149
00:05:05,447 --> 00:05:07,832
a Western, or a Disney picture.

150
00:05:08,012 --> 00:05:09,076
The second one,

151
00:05:09,076 --> 00:05:10,352
after ten o'clock,

152
00:05:10,352 --> 00:05:11,913
was for the teenagers.

153
00:05:12,093 --> 00:05:13,822
Monster movies.

154
00:05:14,002 --> 00:05:16,560
In the fall of fifty-eight there was

155
00:05:16,560 --> 00:05:17,980
one called The Blob,

156
00:05:17,980 --> 00:05:20,751
with a young actor named Steve McQueen,

157
00:05:20,751 --> 00:05:23,237
about a pink jelly from outer space

158
00:05:23,237 --> 00:05:24,800
that ate a whole town.

159
00:05:24,980 --> 00:05:27,360
I had nightmares for a month.

160
00:05:27,710 --> 00:05:29,426
And there was Vertigo,

161
00:05:29,426 --> 00:05:30,829
and South Pacific,

162
00:05:30,829 --> 00:05:32,467
and The Defiant Ones,

163
00:05:32,467 --> 00:05:34,963
and a movie called Thunder Road,

164
00:05:34,963 --> 00:05:38,161
about moonshiners, that every boy in Ohio

165
00:05:38,161 --> 00:05:39,331
saw nine times.

166
00:05:39,511 --> 00:05:41,842
Movies were the biggest thing in the

167
00:05:41,842 --> 00:05:42,878
world back then.

168
00:05:43,058 --> 00:05:45,078
There was no other way to see

169
00:05:45,078 --> 00:05:46,262
a story that big.

170
00:05:46,613 --> 00:05:49,364
Between the two movies there was intermission.

171
00:05:49,544 --> 00:05:52,214
And that's when the hot dogs danced.

172
00:05:52,394 --> 00:05:55,134
A little cartoon came on the screen,

173
00:05:55,134 --> 00:05:56,960
with a hot dog and a box

174
00:05:56,960 --> 00:05:59,624
of popcorn and a candy bar singing,

175
00:05:59,624 --> 00:06:01,603
let's all go to the lobby,

176
00:06:01,603 --> 00:06:03,505
to get ourselves a treat.

177
00:06:03,685 --> 00:06:05,599
And a clock counting down,

178
00:06:05,599 --> 00:06:07,586
ten minutes until showtime.

179
00:06:07,766 --> 00:06:10,215
Every kid in that field begged for

180
00:06:10,215 --> 00:06:10,936
a quarter.

181
00:06:11,286 --> 00:06:13,014
The snack bar was a long low

182
00:06:13,014 --> 00:06:15,174
building in the middle of the field

183
00:06:15,174 --> 00:06:17,149
with the projector booth on top.

184
00:06:17,329 --> 00:06:19,699
Inside it was hot and loud and

185
00:06:19,699 --> 00:06:22,228
smelled like grease and popcorn.

186
00:06:22,408 --> 00:06:25,183
A hot dog was twenty-five cents.

187
00:06:25,363 --> 00:06:27,952
A box of popcorn was a dime.

188
00:06:28,132 --> 00:06:29,734
A Coke was a dime.

189
00:06:29,914 --> 00:06:31,437
And they sold pizza,

190
00:06:31,437 --> 00:06:34,560
which in Ohio in nineteen fifty-eight was

191
00:06:34,560 --> 00:06:36,311
an exotic foreign food.

192
00:06:36,491 --> 00:06:39,661
My father thought it was Italian toast.

193
00:06:40,011 --> 00:06:42,776
Now, the snack bar line was the

194
00:06:42,776 --> 00:06:43,668
real show.

195
00:06:43,848 --> 00:06:46,402
Every family in town was in it,

196
00:06:46,402 --> 00:06:49,862
in their pajamas and curlers and slippers.

197
00:06:50,042 --> 00:06:52,626
You'd see your teacher in her bathrobe

198
00:06:52,626 --> 00:06:53,850
buying a Mars bar.

199
00:06:54,030 --> 00:06:56,432
You'd see the minister's son sneaking a

200
00:06:56,432 --> 00:06:58,279
cigarette behind the building.

201
00:06:58,459 --> 00:07:00,964
It was the only place in Mansfield

202
00:07:00,964 --> 00:07:03,985
where nobody dressed up and nobody cared.

203
00:07:04,336 --> 00:07:06,694
And they sold a little green coil

204
00:07:06,694 --> 00:07:08,837
called PIC that you lit on the

205
00:07:08,837 --> 00:07:11,624
dashboard, and it burned all night with

206
00:07:11,624 --> 00:07:14,125
a thin smoke to keep the mosquitoes

207
00:07:14,125 --> 00:07:14,483
away.

208
00:07:14,663 --> 00:07:17,139
Ohio mosquitoes in July,

209
00:07:17,139 --> 00:07:18,273
sweetheart.

210
00:07:18,453 --> 00:07:20,833
They were the size of sparrows.

211
00:07:21,013 --> 00:07:23,185
That smoke would fill the car,

212
00:07:23,185 --> 00:07:24,706
and Mother would say,

213
00:07:24,706 --> 00:07:26,298
roll your window down,

214
00:07:26,298 --> 00:07:28,688
and the mosquitoes would come in.

215
00:07:28,868 --> 00:07:30,238
You couldn't win.

216
00:07:30,588 --> 00:07:32,062
And if it rained?

217
00:07:32,242 --> 00:07:35,621
Oh, sweetheart, the show went on.

218
00:07:35,801 --> 00:07:38,780
You turned on the windshield wipers every

219
00:07:38,780 --> 00:07:40,161
few minutes, swish,

220
00:07:40,161 --> 00:07:43,068
swish, and watched the movie through the

221
00:07:43,068 --> 00:07:43,649
streaks.

222
00:07:43,829 --> 00:07:46,239
My father kept the engine running so

223
00:07:46,239 --> 00:07:47,912
the defroster would work,

224
00:07:47,912 --> 00:07:50,255
and the whole car smelled like warm

225
00:07:50,255 --> 00:07:51,526
rubber and wet dog.

226
00:07:51,706 --> 00:07:54,172
Some of my favorite movies I only

227
00:07:54,172 --> 00:07:55,816
ever saw through rain.

228
00:07:56,167 --> 00:07:57,974
In the spring and fall,

229
00:07:57,974 --> 00:08:00,018
when Ohio nights got cold,

230
00:08:00,018 --> 00:08:03,947
some drive-ins rented little electric heaters that

231
00:08:03,947 --> 00:08:06,383
plugged in at the speaker pole.

232
00:08:06,563 --> 00:08:07,887
A quarter a night.

233
00:08:08,067 --> 00:08:09,892
You put it on the floor of

234
00:08:09,892 --> 00:08:11,999
the car, and it glowed orange,

235
00:08:11,999 --> 00:08:14,315
and it smelled like burning dust,

236
00:08:14,315 --> 00:08:16,703
and it kept your toes warm through

237
00:08:16,703 --> 00:08:18,458
the whole double feature.

238
00:08:18,638 --> 00:08:21,376
In November the drive-in closed for the

239
00:08:21,376 --> 00:08:24,043
winter, and the big screen stood there

240
00:08:24,043 --> 00:08:26,149
empty in the snow until April.

241
00:08:26,329 --> 00:08:28,501
I used to feel sorry for it.

242
00:08:28,851 --> 00:08:31,347
And here's something you won't believe.

243
00:08:31,527 --> 00:08:33,119
On Sunday mornings,

244
00:08:33,119 --> 00:08:35,718
some drive-ins became churches.

245
00:08:35,898 --> 00:08:38,010
A minister stood on top of the

246
00:08:38,010 --> 00:08:39,982
snack bar with a microphone,

247
00:08:39,982 --> 00:08:43,009
and people listened from their cars through

248
00:08:43,009 --> 00:08:45,685
the speakers, in their Sunday clothes.

249
00:08:45,865 --> 00:08:48,646
A preacher in California started a whole

250
00:08:48,646 --> 00:08:51,844
famous church that way in nineteen fifty-five.

251
00:08:52,024 --> 00:08:55,512
In Mansfield, the Lutherans tried it one

252
00:08:55,512 --> 00:08:56,123
summer.

253
00:08:56,303 --> 00:08:59,126
Grandma Pearl said it wasn't proper to

254
00:08:59,126 --> 00:09:00,761
pray in an Oldsmobile.

255
00:09:01,111 --> 00:09:03,270
Now, the back row.

256
00:09:03,450 --> 00:09:05,555
Your mother will tell me not to

257
00:09:05,555 --> 00:09:06,167
say this.

258
00:09:06,347 --> 00:09:09,010
The back row was where the teenagers

259
00:09:09,010 --> 00:09:09,528
parked.

260
00:09:09,708 --> 00:09:12,256
And the windows of those cars fogged

261
00:09:12,256 --> 00:09:13,035
up so fast,

262
00:09:13,035 --> 00:09:15,866
honey, they couldn't have seen the movie

263
00:09:15,866 --> 00:09:16,999
if they'd tried.

264
00:09:17,179 --> 00:09:19,791
People called the drive-in the passion pit.

265
00:09:19,971 --> 00:09:23,077
There were ushers with flashlights who walked

266
00:09:23,077 --> 00:09:24,802
up and down the back row,

267
00:09:24,802 --> 00:09:26,183
knocking on windows.

268
00:09:26,533 --> 00:09:27,914
In nineteen sixty,

269
00:09:27,914 --> 00:09:29,372
when I was sixteen,

270
00:09:29,372 --> 00:09:31,598
a boy took me to the drive-in

271
00:09:31,598 --> 00:09:34,208
for the very first time without my

272
00:09:34,208 --> 00:09:34,822
parents.

273
00:09:35,002 --> 00:09:36,732
In his own car.

274
00:09:36,912 --> 00:09:38,988
My father made him come in the

275
00:09:38,988 --> 00:09:40,718
house and shake his hand,

276
00:09:40,718 --> 00:09:42,655
and told him to have me home

277
00:09:42,655 --> 00:09:43,832
by eleven-thirty.

278
00:09:44,012 --> 00:09:47,146
The movie didn't end until eleven-forty.

279
00:09:47,326 --> 00:09:49,925
That boy drove me home before the

280
00:09:49,925 --> 00:09:50,240
end.

281
00:09:50,420 --> 00:09:51,593
He was a gentleman.

282
00:09:51,773 --> 00:09:54,362
That boy was your grandfather.

283
00:09:54,542 --> 00:09:56,841
I married him three years later.

284
00:09:57,191 --> 00:09:59,504
At the end of the second movie,

285
00:09:59,504 --> 00:10:02,414
they played the national anthem on some

286
00:10:02,414 --> 00:10:05,548
screens, and a thousand engines started at

287
00:10:05,548 --> 00:10:05,921
once.

288
00:10:06,101 --> 00:10:07,945
The headlights came on,

289
00:10:07,945 --> 00:10:09,709
one row after another,

290
00:10:09,709 --> 00:10:12,676
like a field full of fireflies waking

291
00:10:12,676 --> 00:10:12,916
up.

292
00:10:13,096 --> 00:10:14,993
And the line to get out went

293
00:10:14,993 --> 00:10:16,347
on for half an hour.

294
00:10:16,527 --> 00:10:18,817
My father hummed in the dark the

295
00:10:18,817 --> 00:10:21,393
whole time, with Danny and me asleep

296
00:10:21,393 --> 00:10:22,680
under the blanket.

297
00:10:23,031 --> 00:10:25,822
And that's the moment I remember most,

298
00:10:25,822 --> 00:10:26,630
sweetheart.

299
00:10:26,810 --> 00:10:28,098
Not the movie.

300
00:10:28,278 --> 00:10:29,730
The ride home.

301
00:10:29,910 --> 00:10:32,718
The smell of the cornfields through the

302
00:10:32,718 --> 00:10:34,878
window, the radio playing low,

303
00:10:34,878 --> 00:10:37,398
Mother's head on Father's shoulder.

304
00:10:37,578 --> 00:10:40,256
Feeling the car slow down and turn

305
00:10:40,256 --> 00:10:41,674
into our driveway,

306
00:10:41,674 --> 00:10:44,589
and pretending to be asleep so Father

307
00:10:44,589 --> 00:10:46,007
would carry me in.

308
00:10:46,357 --> 00:10:48,771
So what happened to the drive-ins?

309
00:10:48,951 --> 00:10:51,552
Well, honey, a lot of things.

310
00:10:51,732 --> 00:10:54,449
Daylight saving time pushed the start of

311
00:10:54,449 --> 00:10:56,283
the movies later and later.

312
00:10:56,463 --> 00:10:59,784
Color television came into every living room.

313
00:10:59,964 --> 00:11:02,595
The land under those big fields got

314
00:11:02,595 --> 00:11:05,227
too valuable, right next to the new

315
00:11:05,227 --> 00:11:08,611
highways, and somebody always wanted to build

316
00:11:08,611 --> 00:11:09,588
a mall there.

317
00:11:09,768 --> 00:11:12,276
The Springmill closed in the seventies.

318
00:11:12,456 --> 00:11:14,418
It's a parking lot now.

319
00:11:14,769 --> 00:11:16,809
There are still a few of them,

320
00:11:16,809 --> 00:11:18,782
scattered around the country,

321
00:11:18,782 --> 00:11:20,755
and some even came back for a

322
00:11:20,755 --> 00:11:23,000
little while during the pandemic.

323
00:11:23,180 --> 00:11:25,204
If you ever see one,

324
00:11:25,204 --> 00:11:27,127
sweetheart, you go.

325
00:11:27,307 --> 00:11:29,863
Bring a blanket and a bag of

326
00:11:29,863 --> 00:11:30,593
popcorn.

327
00:11:30,773 --> 00:11:32,433
Wear your pajamas.

328
00:11:32,613 --> 00:11:34,636
And roll your window down,

329
00:11:34,636 --> 00:11:37,048
even if the mosquitoes come in.

330
00:11:37,399 --> 00:11:38,186
Now tell me,

331
00:11:38,186 --> 00:11:40,810
did your grandparents have a drive-in in

332
00:11:40,810 --> 00:11:41,532
their town?

333
00:11:41,712 --> 00:11:43,291
What was it called?

334
00:11:43,471 --> 00:11:45,038
What did they see there?

335
00:11:45,218 --> 00:11:46,916
Write it in the comments,

336
00:11:46,916 --> 00:11:47,935
I want to know.

337
00:11:48,115 --> 00:11:49,732
Come back tomorrow,

338
00:11:49,732 --> 00:11:50,669
sweetheart.

339
00:11:50,849 --> 00:11:51,917
Goodnight.
